From material structure to functional response (@mat-functional-entry: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @materials-engineering, #source "../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Simon-2012.lingua#r_3R4Q71S31MD0EH6YVDQQ2ESKH5", #source "../sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0", #source "../sources/Statistical-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_7FMG47HD1MT706NT3CABRTFNS2", #source "../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_2G2CQ9C5QDEBTQ2YNMYQ5QK3MB", #source "../sources/Functional-Materials-MIT-3-23.lingua#r_3TPN5CK13R844S7PGFK7C1STVN", #source "../sources/Interacting-Electrons-Magnetism-Superconductivity-Ralph.lingua#r_3B28YDT2Z6KYBRZ8DHM1069TW1", #source "../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM", #source "../sources/Fisica-do-Estado-Solido-Assali-IFUSP.lingua#r_3KCKPSW6FY72YA5689VPH8N5PS", #materials-engineering "Complex-amplitudes-eigenvectors-and-observables.lingua#r_1BHW684E94SHWX6QFM759HBP2T") { r_6JHQV78YK5QJAX1C718WZ9945P
**Read progressively:** [Solid State Physics — Steven H. Simon, 2012 draft](../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Simon-2012.lingua#r_3R4Q71S31MD0EH6YVDQQ2ESKH5), chapters 1–22 with exercises and the course homeworks; [Quantum Mechanics — David Tong](../sources/Quantum-Mechanics-Tong.lingua#r_0GY6BHCRA9CR5YGBY1AT9DRZY0) and [Statistical Physics — David Tong](../sources/Statistical-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_7FMG47HD1MT706NT3CABRTFNS2) supply the quantum and statistical explanations when first needed. Then complete [Solid State Physics — David Tong](../sources/Solid-State-Physics-Tong.lingua#r_2G2CQ9C5QDEBTQ2YNMYQ5QK3MB), the available electronic/optical work in [Electrical, Optical, and Magnetic Properties of Materials — MIT 3.23](../sources/Functional-Materials-MIT-3-23.lingua#r_3TPN5CK13R844S7PGFK7C1STVN), and [Electron interactions, magnetism and superconductivity — Daniel C. Ralph](../sources/Interacting-Electrons-Magnetism-Superconductivity-Ralph.lingua#r_3B28YDT2Z6KYBRZ8DHM1069TW1). Use [Functional material response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM) for illustrated mechanisms and [Introdução à Física do Estado Sólido — Lucy V. C. Assali](../sources/Fisica-do-Estado-Solido-Assali-IFUSP.lingua#r_3KCKPSW6FY72YA5689VPH8N5PS) for Portuguese vocabulary and semiconductor explanations.

A wire, thermal barrier, transparent window, capacitor and magnet demand different responses from matter. Begin by describing the imposed field, temperature, frequency, geometry, measured signal and material state. Ask which moving charges, vibrations, dipoles, transitions or collective states could account for that signal.

The route introduces the mathematics and microscopic models before using them. Shared electromagnetic and thermal Records supply their existing explanations. Three later studies connect derivation, independent calculation and primary evidence; completing the reading means being able to defend those accounts, not only recognize property names.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
**Continue in materials engineering:** [Complex amplitudes, eigenvectors and observables](Complex-amplitudes-eigenvectors-and-observables.lingua#r_1BHW684E94SHWX6QFM759HBP2T).

